The solution I used was actually a little different; I use the tty
layout code, and just replace the map view with a tileset. (I need to
customize it so that the map view can expand larger than 80x24,
though, before release.) This means I also get a faketerm build on
Windows for free, which is something that all the ASCII players will
want (Windows' console has insufficient performance to really be
usable).

The big advantage of doing things this way is that the number 1 thing I
hate most about NetHack tiles ports is that they change all the key
responses and interface layout from the tty port for no good reason. I
hate having to use return rather than space in menus, or maybe escape
instead of return; little things like that completely screw up my muscle
memory and make the tiles port a misery to use. Thus, I wanted to make
it as similar to the tty port as possible; let the difference between
tiles and ASCII be one of how the dungeon is drawn, not one of what all
the controls and keybindings are. (Not that I'm opposed to adding
togglable support for, say, mouse clicks to the tiles build, or indeed
the ASCII build; the aim is to not break existing interfaces, rather
than to prevent people building new ones.)

